      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cohutta was once called 'Shakerag,' a nickname born when rag‑clad passengers waved to stop passing trains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cohutta%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sprang up as a railroad stop between Cleveland, Tennessee, and Dalton, Georgia, becoming a regional trade hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cohutta%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Red Clay State Historic Park in Cohutta stands as the last seat of the Cherokee Nation and a national Native American meeting ground.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofcohuttaga.com/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cohutta Fish Hatchery, now run by the University of Georgia, houses a visitors’ center and aquarium that showcase local trout breeding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cohutta%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Annual events like the Ruritan Club Chicken‑Q and a historic Christmas parade keep Cohutta’s small‑town spirit alive each year.",
